It appears that I don't accept your definition of censorship, nor you
mine.  I have not hypothesized any restriction upon what people say.
Since I see the 'audience' as 'Usenet', I have not even restricted
where they say it.  I have simply asked that they categorize it
appropriately.

>If you attempt to interfere with their access to any part of Hyde Park
>Corner, you will be the person ejected by the police, not the speaker.

Do you not spot your inconsistency here?  If they try to 'access' a bit
of Hyde Park Corner which is *already being used* by another speaker and
his group of listeners, then they are, in fact, interfering with the
access of that group, and are likely to be reined in.  This is the
analogy that I see.

>The PA argument again?  I am not going to refute that argument one more
>time.  There is nothing analogous on Usenet to sound.  Not unless someone
>UUEncodes a .wav file.

Don't be so literal, that was a metaphor.  

>No one has more free speech rights than another.  Not in Hyde Park.  Not in
>an unmoderated Usenet newsgroup.  Spamming does not violate that principle.
>It is nothing more than trying to reach a large number of audiences with
>your message.  The audience can then do what they want with it.  Read it.
>Ignore it.  Consider it.  Adopt it.  Argue with it.  The only thing they
>cannot legitimately do is try to suppress it.  If they want to limit
>discussion, the appropriate choice is for them to go off and form a closed
>group all their own (i.e., a moderated Usenet group).

But this is what even un-moderated groups ARE.  They are groups of
people who have opted to go off and form a group for discussion related
to a particular topic.  Why do they have to put someone (a moderator)
in charge?  Sorry, but your argument for 'freedom' is actually beginning
to involve too much control to make me comfortable.

>No.  It does not.  Because you propose that some speakers be placed off
>the beaten path, where no one will come along and even realize they are
>there.  

No, I am not proposing that they be 'placed off the beaten path'.  I am
proposing that they be placed where the advertisements have said that
their topic will be discussed, and where those people who are
interested are standing, waiting for them to come along and say what
they want to say.  If I am interested in (for example) stupid bicycle
safety laws, you don't need to post in news.admin.misc to reach me,
because I will also be in rec.bicycles.* waiting for your postings.
If I wanted a green card (being a US citizen still, I don't) I would
be watching alt.visa.us, and so I don't need an ad in comp.lang.c.

(This is why I have some hopes for the 'education' approach.  Proper
selection of groups increases the probability of reaching those people
who are interested, and decreases the probability of pissing off people
who might be sympathetic, but who lose it when you try to jam your
message down their throats.  It benefits the speaker as well as the
listener.)

