In article <2vmaou$53d@mailer.fsu.edu>,
Mitch  <gmitchel@garnet.acns.fsu.edu> wrote:
>
><Benjamin.J.Tilly@dartmouth.edu> writes:
>> 
>> What if the upstream sites also went down? If a substantial portion of
>> the net goes down at once then messages will be lost. Spamming has the
>> potential to do that .
>>
>Sites are generally upstream and downstream at the same time.  Usenet is
>not some hierarchical system with an ultimate upstream site(s).
>
No but most leaf sites have only one upstream site.  Thus if that upstream
site goes down, the leaf site loses all incoming news until their feed is
back up.  Also, if the site itself fills temporarily, the upstream site may
not hold the unsent news long enough for the affected site to receive it.

>The messages are processed on a "I have . . ., I need . . ." basis.  When
>a system throttles, it can send out requests (i.e., "I need . . .") to other
>sites.
>
Wrong, there is no such thing as an "I need" command.  You are thinking of
IHAVE/SENDME.  A feed (NNTP mostly) offers a peer a message via IHAVE, if
the peer wants it, it issues a SENDME.  There is no way for a site to
request individual messages it does not know about.  Sites using NNTP can
issue the NEWNEWS command to generate a list of Message-ID's, compare them
to it's history database and then issue appropriate GROUP and ARTICLE
commands to suck over messages but this is not the normal procedure for a
newsfeed.  A site cannot, as far as I understand how NNTP and INN work,
issue a SENDME unless the feed site has used IHAVE.  NEWNEWS, GROUP and
ARTICLE commands are normally only used by reader clients, not news peers.

Second, unless a site already has multiple feeds, it cannot connect to
another site.  Again, even in the case of multiple feeds, the feeding site
has, as a minimum, batched up the Message-IDs of articles it wishes to send
to the downstream site.  There are no NNTP systems I am aware of that cache
Message-IDs of articles offered by it's various feeds when it throttles due
to space limits and then queries each of it's feeds to see which might still
have the articles.

In the case of UUCP newsfeeds, it works a little differently.  The entire
article is stored in a batch and compressed for transmission.  The use of
the IHAVE/SENDME control messages is rare because of the inherent delays
this produces.  If the receiving site of a UUCP feed runs out of space, the
unsent batches are held by the sending site subject to the purging rules
for unsent batches in the UUCP spool area of the sending site.

>Most sites feed multiple sites and in turn are fed by multiple sites.  Usenet
>is designed to propogate messages in the event that some site goes offline.
>A significant proportion of the net would have to go offline simultaneously
>to prevent message propogation.
>
Most large sites are set up this way but the thousands of small or
individual sites have a single feed and do not feed anyone else.  There are
also quite a few pass thru sites with but a single feed.

>
>> Depends on the quantity and number of sources of it. And if it reaches
>> a high enough volume to cause problems with news propagation (which C&S
>> did) then it will affect how well you can see other speakers. So the
>> analogy is IMO fairly sound.
>>
>What caused a problem in the C&S case was all of the cyber censors rushing
>out to mailbomb the C&S site in an organized attempt to block C&S access to
>the network.
>
In my case I strongly suspect that the volume spurt caused by C&S did
indeed create problems for me.  I had the news to a UUCP leaf site stall
which in turn caused the spool area to fill.  I had another server run out
of inodes.  None of these sites have these problems under normal news flow
conditions given the expire and newsgroup policies in place at these sites.  
I agree that this is my problem but present it to show that massive
postings in a very short time do stress portions of the infrastructure.
Because I had to actually delete the unsent news, it could be argued that
what C&S did in fact deprived others of a portion of their audience that
they would otherwise have reached.

>Spamming poses no threat to the infrastructure of the net at all.

It increases the resources needed by individual sites for participation in
Usenet much faster than if spamming did not occur.  It may force sites
which cannot obtain increased resources to further curtail what they do
carry or to drop out all together.  Unrestricted spamming will cause these
events to take place much sooner than they would under the normal growth of
Usenet.  This may be preceived by some as a threat to the infrastructure.

Mike
