That is your opinion.  While Usenet may not be exactly analogous to a
library, it is far from analogous to Hyde Park Corner.  Hyde Park Corner
does not have areas which either by design or tradition are set aside for a
particular topic.  Whomever gets to a spot first can start talking on
whatever they wish to.  Even if one speaker routinely uses the same spot
for his speech, if someone else were to get there first, the previous user
of that spot can not demand the new speaker to give up his place.  Usenet
by design has established places for discussions on a particular topic.
While the occasional off topic posting is tolerated, habitual off topic
posters are directed elsewhere with varying degrees of persuasion.

The fact that Hyde Park Corner has sufficient space to accomodate multiple
speakers on different topics without serious interference with each other
does not make it the same as Usenet with it's pre-established topic areas.
Unless Hyde Park Corner starts labeling various sections as the appropriate
place for a given discussion, it is not analogous to Usenet and it's
newsgroups.

