BULLSHIT!!!  That is precisely what UUNET, PSI and many other commercial
providers sell.  A Usenet newsfeed only account is precisely what anatolia.org
purchased from UUNET.  Not shell access, not e-mail, not the ability to
read news, but precisely a Usenet newsfeed.  Many of their customers have just 
such accounts.  They pay for a uni- or bidirectional newsfeed, they pay
specificly for the right to receive news on their machine and to send news
from their machine.  Even those using NNTP read/post clients via SLIP can
obtain an account soley for that purpose.  This is exactly the same as
purchasing the clarinet.* newsgroups from Brad Templeton.

>
>This argument about some private site deciding what messages to pass along
>to other sites is disturbing.  It means that some sites are not deciding
>for themselves what messages they wish to post on their own machines, they
>are deciding which messages other machines can post.  It is also an
>argument that is based predominantly on ignorance of how Usenet messages
>propagate.  Usenet messages propagate on the basis of, "I have . . ., I
>want . . ." and not "I want . . ., will you let me have . . .".  If some
>site does not have a certain message (perhaps because the site does not
>carry a certain Usenet group), then a site that wants the message can look
>elsewhere.  Ordinarily, it will have multiple sources available for any given 
>message.

Once again you are showing your ignorance of how Usenet messages propagate.
There is no such thing as an "I want ...".  There is a SENDME in response
to an IHAVE.  If my feed(s) does not carry alt.foo.bar there is no magical
way for me to poll hundreds of different sites looking for alt.foo.bar.
One of my feeds must have it and offer the articles to me.  If I tried to
poll other sites I would get an error message when I attempted to connect
saying that I was not permitted access to that server.  Some sites permit
almost anyone to submit an article via the the IHAVE command and some
permit open reading.  These do not constitute a news peer relationship
which must be established by the two sites in question.  Even those sites
which permit anyone to submit via IHAVE do not permit random sites to suck
news from them.

>It is very chilling that some system administrators asert the right to
>suppress messages from propogating around the network.  The system
>administrator can decide what a site will receive, but a system administrator
>should not be able to decide what other sites will receive.

They do not assert that right.  What is asserted is that you can get from
them what newsgroups they are willing to offer to you, with no guarantee
that what they are offering you is complete.  What is also asserted is that
each site has the right to chose what messages are carried and passed via
that site.  If I were to place restrictions on what I receive and therefor 
restrictions on what I send downstream, courtesy would dictate that I inform 
the admins of the sites I feed.  They are then free to either try and convince 
me to change what I receive or find another site that is less restricted in 
what it receives.  I am also free to do the same if I find I do not agree
with the policies of my feed(s).

