Last week, Mitch made a few assertions that .mil/.gov/.edu sites 
outnumber .com/.net sites; he used the assertion, after a fashion,
to support his contention that Usenet required a rather absolutist
First Amendment support.

I decided to go digging; those of you who have read my postings in
the past know that I have this near-obsession with actual facts.  8)

Surprisingly, I could not find a specific count of the top-level do-
mains.  I expected to find *something* that said, "This month, there
are XXX domains registered under COM, YYY under EDU, ..."  No such
document appears to exist, according to the folks at rs.internic.net.
(InterNIC is the central point for the TCP/IP internet; they are the
subdomain registry for several major domains.) 

Digging around, I was pointed to InterNIC's Domain Contacts List.
For every domain registered, this gives the contact person.  At this
point, I made my first assumption - namely, that most of the subdo-
mains relevant to our discussion are registered.  I did find, however, 
that .MIL sites are not included in this list; a moment's thought 
should give the reader several reasons for this omission.  I then
made a further assumption - namely, that anyone running an unregis-
tered domain is beneath our notice in this question.

Anyway, I retrieved this file (domain-contacts.txt, via anonymous
ftp from rs.internic.net, filespec /pub/domain/domain-contacts.txt)
and deleted all but the actual domain names.  I then generated the
following counts:

	.COM  16404              .EDU   1513
	.NET    773              .GOV    278
        -----------              -----------
              17177                     1791

Two other domains of interest:
	.US     270		 .ORG   1663

These domains are something of an amalgam; they can include public,
private and quasi-governmental domains.  For instance, many public
school systems are registering in the .US domain.

At first glance, this would seem to indicate that, in terms of actual
registered domains, the commercial and private sector outnumbers the
government and academic sector by a factor of almost 10 to 1.

(Note: this list does not include subdomains of international domains, 
like .DE or .CH; those domains are managed by sources in their respective 
countries.  This list only included those domains for which InterNIC is 
responsible.)

While I certainly recognize a healthy margin of error, it would seem
that the private and commercial domains greatly outnumber governmental 
and quasi-governmental domains.  I also doubt that even MILnet could
recover a 10 to 1 margin by itself.  If anyone knows of a more authori-
tative source, please let me know.

The Constitutional questions being debated here do not apply to indi-
vidual sites; it is at the domain boundaries (uky.edu vs. bga.com vs.
vnet.net) that these questions become an issue.  Therefore, the total 
number of hosts in a given domain is irrelevant; it is the *domain* 
count with which we should be concerned.  These statistics, limited 
though they may be, show a distinct numerical superiority for commercial 
and private domains.  

It would seem, therefore, that Mitch's assertion concerning the primacy
of government-funded sites on the Internet, as we know it today, is false.

Comments?

--Wes

