From caf-talk Caf Jul 14 00:01:28 1994
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: cmagwood@TrentU.CA
Subject: Call for submissions for book about censorship in Canada
Message-ID: <1994Jul14.035206.13761@blaze.trentu.ca>
Date: Thu, 14 Jul 1994 03:52:06 GMT

I am currently working on editing a book about censorship in
Canada, and am looking for contributions from Canadians, or from
others who have close links with the Canadian experience of
censorship.
The book will include voices that span the spectrum of thought
regarding censorship, from the vehemently anti-censorship position
of civil libertarians to the vehemently pro-censorship position of
extreme religious groups, as well as the more moderate views to be
found in between.
I envision the book containing opinion pieces by spokespersons who
are involved in censorship cases, historical pieces, surveys of
obscenity law, manifestos and tracts by groups pro and anti,
interviews with past and present policy-makers, judges (past only,
I guess), and law enforcers, and essays by famous writers and
celebrities (and regular folk too!) on the topic: What I Would
Censor.
Censorship topics would include debates surrounding: banning
school texts, television violence, religious headgear, Canada
Customs and book bannings, political correctness, obscenity law,
multiculturalism, zero tolerance government policies, neo-Nazism,
art exhibits, and more.
Unlike the American example, in which all these arguments are
framed in the context of constitutional protection of "freedom of
speech", Canadian society and Canadian law are much more ambiguous
about the issue, and our arguments are more wildly variable as a
result.
If you have any ideas about submissions that you would like to
discuss, or if you have completed work that would be of interest,
or if you have suggetions for research that I might pursue, please
contact me at cmagwood@trentu.ca.

