I don't know if we're talking about the same thing here... the
"prosecutor's brief," the only-the-parts-that-support-our-position may
be what the university releases to the public, but that in no way
indicates that the "internal brief" -- the report written by university
counsel and submitted to the school's Powers That Be -- will not contain
a full analysis of the cons as well as the pros.

And while it may be true that "it's always difficult to bear bad news
and tell the boss not to do something," I suspect that it's even harder
to be the one who, after the shit-hammer falls, has to explain to the
bosses why you didn't warn them about all the potential risks and
pitfalls back in your original report way back when...

> Implicit in the discussion is that the question is phrased along the
