> type of "prosecution's brief".

Of course... but it also, implicitly, contains a point-by-point
discussion of all the valid objections that the other side can raise,
and if the writer is competent, it will, for each of those points,
contain not only "our counter-argument" but also a thumbnail analysis of
how well or poorly that counter-arg is likely to stand up legally if the
matter goes into litigation.

> Perhaps I am wrong, but I would be astounded if a student could go to
> a university counsel's office and get legal help in challenging one of
> the university's policies. Maybe a naive employee would actually work
> on this, but I severely doubt such a person would be around for long.

You're not wrong, but the question is... :-)  The student would get no
such legal help for the simple reason that it would constitute a
professional conflict of interest for the university counsel.  An
attorney simply _cannot_ represent two clients who are on opposite sides
in a matter, at least not without the explicit informed consent of both,
and even then it's shaky.

-- William December Starr <wdstarr@crl.com>


